%% Sequence diagram — Contact form submission via the Telegram Bot API
%% Pseudo-level: the Telegram call is the only server-side/dynamic flow in the site.
sequenceDiagram
    actor Visitor
    participant Form as ContactForm (client component)
    participant Route as /api/contact (Next.js API route)
    participant Env as Server env (BOT_TOKEN, CHAT_ID)
    participant Telegram as Telegram Bot API (api.telegram.org)

    Visitor->>Form: Fill name, message, pick callback method (WhatsApp/phone, Telegram, or Email) from a selector, enter matching contact value, submit
    Form->>Form: Client-side validation (required fields; value format checked against the selected method)
    Form->>Route: POST { name, callbackMethod, callbackValue, message } (HTTPS, no secrets in payload)
    activate Route
    Route->>Route: Validate & sanitize body server-side (callbackMethod is an enum: whatsapp | telegram | email)
    alt validation fails
        Route-->>Form: 400 + error reason
        Form-->>Visitor: Show inline error, keep entered values
    else validation passes
        Route->>Env: Read BOT_TOKEN / CHAT_ID
        Route->>Telegram: POST https://api.telegram.org/bot{BOT_TOKEN}/sendMessage (chat_id=CHAT_ID, text=composed message incl. name, chosen callback method + value, and the visitor's message)
        activate Telegram
        alt Telegram accepts
            Telegram-->>Route: 2xx (message sent)
            Route-->>Form: 200 success
            Form-->>Visitor: Show "message sent" confirmation
        else Telegram/network error
            Telegram-->>Route: error / timeout
            Route-->>Form: 502 + generic error (no Telegram internals leaked)
            Form-->>Visitor: Show "try again later" + fallback to email/Telegram handle
        end
        deactivate Telegram
    end
    deactivate Route

    note over Route,Env: BOT_TOKEN never reaches the browser — read only<br/>inside the API route, which runs server-side only.
